{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050267 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096669 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096456 :question/math-academy-id "e-4690" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+z &=3 \\\\ y+z &=2 \\\\ z &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that $z=2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+(2)=2 \n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\ty = 2-2 = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute both $z=2$ and $y=0$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+(0)+(2)=3\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx = 3-2 = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x = 1,$ $y=0,$ and $z = 2.$ Or, in vector form, the solution is $\\langle 1,0,2 \\rangle.$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097178 :question/math-academy-id "q-20418" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=3 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-(2)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}+2=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}$ and ${x}_{3}=2$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(\\frac{5}{2})-(2)=3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3+2-\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{2}$, ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}$, and ${x}_{3}=2.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5}{2},2⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097182 :question/math-academy-id "q-20451" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x_{1} + 3x_{2} - 2x_{3} &=-5 \\\\ x_{2} - x_{3} &=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x_{3} &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237857 :question/math-academy-id "q-10195" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+z &=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y+z &=1 \\\\ z &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that  $z=2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get \n\n$$\ny+2=1\\,⟹\\,y=1-2=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=2$ and $y=-1$into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+(-1)+(2)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}-2+1=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$, $y=-1$, and $z=2.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨-\\frac{1}{2},-1,2⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237858 :question/math-academy-id "q-10196" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=-1 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=3.$ Substitute this into the second equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-(3)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-1+3=2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=3$ and ${x}_{2}=2$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(2)+(3)=1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=1-3+2=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=0$,  ${x}_{2}=2$, and ${x}_{3}=3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨0,2,3⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237860 :question/math-academy-id "q-10197" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y-z &=4 \\\\ y+2z &=2 \\\\ z &=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "he third equation states that  $z=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+2(\\frac{1}{2})=2\\,⟹\\,y=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $y=1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx-(1)-(\\frac{1}{2})=4\\,⟹\\,x=4+1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{11}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\frac{11}{2}$,  $y=1$, and $z=\\frac{1}{2}.$  In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨\\frac{11}{2},1,\\frac{1}{2}⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237861 :question/math-academy-id "q-10198" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=9 \\\\ {x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that  ${x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-3(\\frac{1}{3})=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=2+1=3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(3)+3(\\frac{1}{3})=9\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=9+3-1=11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=11$, ${x}_{2}=3$, and ${x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨11,3,\\frac{1}{3}⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237863 :question/math-academy-id "q-10199" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y-z &=3 \\\\ y-z &=2 \\\\ z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that $z=1.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny-(1)=2\\,⟹\\,y=2+1=3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=1$ and $y=3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx-(3)-(1)=3\\,⟹\\,x=3+4=7.\n$$\n\nThus, the solution is $x=7$,  $y=3$,  and $z=1.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨7,3,1⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265729 :question/math-academy-id "q-129447" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(-1)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}+1=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${x}_{3}=-1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(\\frac{1}{2})+(-1)=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2+1+1=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=4$, ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}$, and ${x}_{3}=-1.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨4,\\frac{1}{2},-1⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265730 :question/math-academy-id "q-129452" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y-z &=2 \\\\ y-z &=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that $z=3.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny-3=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}+3=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $y=\\frac{7}{2}$ and $z=3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+\\frac{7}{2}-3=2\\,⟹\\,x=2-\\frac{7}{2}+3=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\frac{3}{2}$, $y=\\frac{7}{2}$, and $z=3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265731 :question/math-academy-id "q-129453" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=4 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=-2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(-2)=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=2+2=4.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=4$ and ${x}_{3}=-2$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-4-(-2)=4\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=4+4-2=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=6$, ${x}_{2}=4$, and ${x}_{3}=-2.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨6,4,-2⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265732 :question/math-academy-id "q-129455" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}-6{x}_{3} &=3 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-6(-\\frac{1}{3})=3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=3-2=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+1-(-\\frac{1}{3})=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2-1-\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}$, ${x}_{2}=1$, and ${x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413953 :question/math-academy-id "q-20421" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y-z &=4 \\\\ y+z &=2 \\\\ z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that $z=3.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get  \n\n$$\ny+(3)=2\\,⟹\\,y=2-3=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we substitute $z=3$ and $y=-1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx-(-1)-(3)=4\\,⟹\\,x=4-1+3=6.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=6$, $y=-1$, and $z=3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations Using Back Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096670 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096457 :question/math-academy-id "e-4691" :question/problem "Solve the system of equations that is represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5\\end{array} \n\t\\right].\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x_1,$ $x_2,$ and $x_3.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that $x_3=5.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2+(5)=-2\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx_2=-2-5=-7.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute both $x_3 = 5$ and $x_2 = -7$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx_1+(-7)-(5) = 1\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx_1 = 1 + 7 + 5 = 13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x_1 = 13,$ $x_2=-7,$ and $x_3 = 5.$ Or, in vector form, the solution is $\\langle 13,-7,5 \\rangle.$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096925 :question/math-academy-id "q-10205" :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+z &=1 \\\\ y+z &=1 \\\\ z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that $z=1.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+(1)=1\\,⟹\\,y=1-1=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=1$ and $y=0$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+(0)+(1)=1\\,⟹\\,x=1-1=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=0$, $y=0$, and $z=1.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨0,0,1⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097179 :question/math-academy-id "q-20422" :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say,  ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=5 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that ${x}_{3}=2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(2)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=-2$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3(-2)+4(2)=5\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=5-6-8=-9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=-9$, ${x}_{2}=-2$, and ${x}_{3}=2.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨-9,-2,2⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265733 :question/math-academy-id "q-129457" :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{3} & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say,  ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}+\\frac{{x}_{3}}{3} &=-1 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that ${x}_{3}=2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+\\frac{2}{3}=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-1-\\frac{2}{3}=-\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{3}$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+3(-\\frac{5}{3})+(2)=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2+5-2=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=5$, ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{3}$, and ${x}_{3}=2.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨5,-\\frac{5}{3},2⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265734 :question/math-academy-id "q-129459" :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+3z &=-2 \\\\ y+4z &=0 \\\\ z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that $z=3.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+4(3)=0\\,⟹\\,y=-12.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=3$ and $y=-12$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+(-12)+3(3)=-2\\,⟹\\,x=-2+12-9=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=1$, $y=-12$, and $z=3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨1,-12,3⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265749 :question/math-academy-id "q-129569" :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say,  ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=3 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(-1)=3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=3+1=4.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=4$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(4)+3(-1)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=8+3=11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=11$, ${x}_{2}=4$, and ${x}_{3}=-1.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨11,4,-1⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265750 :question/math-academy-id "q-129599" :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+2z &=-1 \\\\ y+3z &=2 \\\\ z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that $z=3.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+3(3)=2\\,⟹\\,y=2-9=-7.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=3$ and $y=-7$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+(-7)+2(3)=-1\\,⟹\\,x=-1+7-6=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=0$, $y=-7$, and $z=3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨0,-7,3⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287990 :question/math-academy-id "q-20452" :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say,  ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=-1 \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that  ${x}_{3}=-3.$ Substituting this into the second equation,  we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+2(-3)=-2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-2-2(-3)=-2+6=4.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=-3$ and ${x}_{2}=4$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(4)+3(-3)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-1-8+9=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=0$, ${x}_{2}=4$, and ${x}_{3}=-3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341889 :question/math-academy-id "q-72972" :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+z &=-8 \\\\ y+2z &=-1 \\\\ z &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that $z=2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+2(2)=-1\\,⟹\\,y=-1-4=-5.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=2$ and $y=-5$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+2(-5)+(2)=-8\\,⟹\\,x=-8+10-2=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=0$, $y=-5$, and $z=2.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨0,-5,2⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341890 :question/math-academy-id "q-72978" :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say,  ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=-3 \\\\ {x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=-4 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that  ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting this into the second equation,  we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+3(-1)=-4\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-4+3=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=-1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(-1)+4(-1)=-3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-3+1+4=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=2$, ${x}_{2}=-1$, and ${x}_{3}=-1.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations Using Back Substitution Given an Augmented Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096671 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096458 :question/math-academy-id "e-4692" :question/problem "Solve the system of equations that is represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 3\\end{array} \n\t\\right].\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x_1,$ $x_2,$ and $x_3.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ 3{x}_{3} &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that\n\n$$\n3x_3=3 \n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \n\tx_3=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2+(1)=-2\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx_2=-2-1=-3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute both $x_3 = 1$ and $x_2 = -3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n2x_1+(-3)-(1) = 0\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx_1 = \\dfrac{1}{2} (3 + 1) = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x_1 = 2,$ $x_2=-3,$ and $x_3 = 1.$ Or, in vector form, the solution is $\\langle 2,-3,1 \\rangle.$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096923 :question/math-academy-id "q-10202" :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix:\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096924 :question/math-academy-id "q-10204" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-z &=10 \\\\ y+5z &=15 \\\\ -z &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that \n\n$$\n-z=5\\,⟹\\,z=-5.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+5(-5)=15\\,⟹\\,y=15+25=40.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=-5$ and $y=40$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+2(40)-(-5)=10\\,⟹\\,x=10-80-5=-75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-75,y=40$ and $z=-5.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨-75,40,-5⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237870 :question/math-academy-id "q-10203" :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y+z &=8 \\\\ y-z &=-1 \\\\ z &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that $z=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny-0=-1\\,⟹\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=0$ and $y=-1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n2x-2(-1)+(0)=8\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{8-2}{2}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=3$, $y=-1$, and $z=0.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨3,-1,0⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265751 :question/math-academy-id "q-129604" :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix: \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+y+2z &=-3 \\\\ y+3z &=0 \\\\ -z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that \n\n$$\n-z=1\\,⟹\\,z=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+3(-1)=0\\,⟹\\,y=3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=-1$ and $y=3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n4x+(3)+2(-1)=-3\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{-3-3+2}{4}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1$, $y=3$, and $z=-1.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as \n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265752 :question/math-academy-id "q-129613" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=-1 \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=-3 \\\\ -{x}_{3} &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that \n\n$$\n-{x}_{3}=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{3}=1.\n$$\n\n Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+2(1)=-3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-3-2=-5.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=1$ and ${x}_{2}=-5$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}-(-5)+4(1)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{-1-5-4}{2}=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is \n${x}_{1}=-5$, ${x}_{2}=-5$, and ${x}_{3}=1.$  In vector form, the solution can be written as $$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265753 :question/math-academy-id "q-129615" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-1 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=7 \\\\ 2{x}_{3} &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that \n\n$$\n2{x}_{3}=4\\,⟹\\,{x}_{3}=2.\n$$\n\n Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-{x}_{2}+3(2)=7\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=6-7=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=-1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+5(-1)+(2)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-1+5-2=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is \n${x}_{1}=2$, ${x}_{2}=-1$, and ${x}_{3}=2.$  In vector form, the solution can be written as $$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341891 :question/math-academy-id "q-72980" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y-z &=2 \\\\ y+3z &=6 \\\\ -z &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that\n\n$$\n-z=-3\\,⟹\\,z=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+3(3)=6\\,⟹\\,y=6-9=-3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=3$ and $y=-3$ into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n2x+(-3)-(3)=2\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}(2+3+3)=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=4$, $y=-3$ and $z=3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨4,-3,3⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341892 :question/math-academy-id "q-72981" :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix: \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+z &=2 \\\\ 3y+2z &=-1 \\\\ -z &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that\n\n$$\n-z=2\\,⟹\\,z=-2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n3y+2(-2)=-1\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}(-1+4)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=-2$ and $y=1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+(-2)=2\\,⟹\\,x=2+2=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=4$, $y=1$, and $z=-2.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as \n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341893 :question/math-academy-id "q-72983" :question/problem "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y+z &=-1 \\\\ y+z &=1 \\\\ 2z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that\n \n\n$$\n2z=1\\,⟹\\,z=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+(\\frac{1}{2})=1\\,⟹\\,y=1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=y=\\frac{1}{2}$  into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n2x+(\\frac{1}{2})+(\\frac{1}{2})=-1\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}(-1-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1$, $y=\\frac{1}{2}$, and $z=\\frac{1}{2}.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨-1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations Given an Augmented Matrix Containing Non-Unit Leading Entries"}] :topic/title "Solving Systems of Equations Using Back Substitution"}
